 # Multi-view projection
 ## Overview
-Multi-view projection voxelization converts a triangle mesh into a 3D voxel grid by rasterizing the geometry from multiple camera viewpoints, typically aligned with the principal axes of the scene's bounding box [G]. It solves the problem of occlusion gaps and missing data inherent in single-view projections by ensuring volumetric coverage from multiple directions [G]. It differs from [Single 2D Projection Voxelization](3482-single-2d-projection-voxelization.md) by using multiple views to cover all surface normals, and from [3D Grid Rasterization](903-3d-grid-rasterization.md) by relying on standard GPU rasterization pipelines rather than explicit 3D grid traversal [G].
+Multi-view projection voxelization converts a triangle mesh into a 3D voxel grid by rasterizing geometry from multiple camera viewpoints aligned with the scene's bounding box axes [G]. It solves occlusion gaps from single-view projections by ensuring volumetric coverage, differing from [Single 2D Projection Voxelization](3482-single-2d-projection-voxelization.md) by using multiple views for all normals, and from [3D Grid Rasterization](903-3d-grid-rasterization.md) by relying on standard GPU rasterization rather than explicit 3D traversal [G].
